<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Transfer — Load (stepped capacity verification)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Load test: verify the capacity BOUNDARY discovered by the transfer
stress test holds under sustained load — not just a 30s rolling window.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from sustained-capacity stress run, +2/30s):  28 VUs       ║
║                                                                      ║
║   BOUNDARY      =  28 VUs   (last fully clean step in the +2/30s     ║
║                              stress run with cap 50 and 30s hold     ║
║                              per step; 30 VUs returned 17% errors    ║
║                              within the 30s window and 32 VUs was    ║
║                              100% errors — P2028 commit timeouts)    ║
║   70% BOUNDARY  =  20 VUs   (warm-up / recovery level)               ║
║                                                                      ║
║ The boundary search took three stress shapes to converge:            ║
║   Run 1 (+50/10s, cap 2000): clean to 300, 100% at 450 → transient   ║
║   Run 2 (+10/10s, cap 200):  clean to 60,  100% at 110 → still       ║
║                              transient; first sized this load plan   ║
║                              against B=60 and the run collapsed at   ║
║                              42 VUs (70% of B) within 30s.           ║
║   Run 3 (+2/30s,  cap  50):  clean to 28,  100% at 32  → SUSTAINED   ║
║                                                                      ║
║ The transfer write path holds row locks inside a Prisma              ║
║ $transaction (FOR UPDATE on source and dest accounts, two INSERTs    ║
║ on transactions). Under sustained concurrency the lock queue grows   ║
║ linearly until commits exceed Prisma's default 5s timeout, then      ║
║ every transaction in the queue ages out simultaneously (P2028).      ║
║ 30s per step is the minimum hold that exposes this; shorter holds    ║
║ measure thread-start throughput, not steady-state capacity.          ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Brackets are ±~4% of B (vs ±~5% in the other load plans). At         ║
║ this scale ±5% rounds to ±1 VU at the lower edge and ±2 at the       ║
║ upper, which is fine — the spacing still flanks B without leaving    ║
║ a gap:                                                               ║
║                                                                      ║
║   warm-up         =  20 VUs   ( 71% — same as recovery)              ║
║   just-under      =  27 VUs   ( 96% — confirms approach is stable)   ║
║   at boundary     =  28 VUs   (100% — the 10-min hold)               ║
║   just-over       =  29 VUs   (104% — should be near-clean;          ║
║                                       30 was the first error step)   ║
║   recovery        =  20 VUs   ( 71%)                                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Load-generator memory: 29 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈ 29 MB     ║
║ peak. Trivial; default JMeter heap (1 GB) is fine.                   ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

This is a HYBRID load test — a stepped capacity scan with recovery — not a
single-level steady-state load test. Each step holds long enough to expose
slow degradation (lock-queue growth, connection-pool starvation, fraud
table growth).

Virtual users are spread across MANY distinct (source, destination) pairs
(tests/stress/data/transfers.csv, 1000 rows) via CSVDataSet round-robin
(shareMode.all + recycle). The seed script pairs source[i] -&gt; dest[(i +
SHIFT) % N] with SHIFT coprime with N near N/2 (499 for N=1000) — every
account is source of exactly one row and destination of exactly one
(different) row, so the lock graph spreads across the full account
population and adjacent CSV rows share no accounts.

This exercises the FULL WRITE path: FOR UPDATE on TWO accounts inside a
$transaction, TWO transaction rows written (TRANSFER_OUT + TRANSFER_IN),
fraud detector runs. The Prisma connection pool, the Postgres lock
manager, and the fraud detector's velocity queries are the most likely
saturation points — surfaced as P2028 transaction-timeout 500s when the
pool blocks.

Shape (Ultimate Thread Group, 28 min total):

  ┌──────────────┬──────┬───────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ VUs  │ Hold  │ Maps to spec                    │
  ├──────────────┼──────┼───────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │   20 │  5 m  │ 71% of boundary    (warm-up)    │
  │ 5–10 min     │   27 │  5 m  │ 96% of boundary    (just under) │
  │ 10–20 min    │   28 │ 10 m  │ boundary           (at boundary)│
  │ 20–25 min    │   29 │  5 m  │ 104% of boundary   (just over)  │
  │ 25–28 min    │   20 │  3 m  │ 71% of boundary    (recovery)   │
  └──────────────┴──────┴───────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria per phase (same gates as stress):
  - p95 latency &lt; 5000ms
  - error rate &lt; 1%

Response Assertion: regex ^(2\d\d|422)$ — for the transfer endpoint,
both 2xx (200 completed / 201 PENDING_WEEKEND queued) and 422 (business
rule rejection like DAILY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED) are valid responses. Only
5xx, connect/read timeouts, and other transport errors count as
failures. assume_success=false so transport-level errors are preserved
as errors (matching the transfer stress plan).

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms — total delay ≈ 3000ms + |N(0, 1000)| per
iteration). Models realistic user pacing: a customer initiates a
transfer, then continues with other UI flows. Without it each VU would
loop as fast as the server replies, which is a saturation pattern, not
a load pattern.

⚠ Weekend caveat (banking-rules §4): transfers initiated on Saturday or
Sunday UTC are routed to PENDING_WEEKEND (status 201, queued for Monday
09:00 UTC) and do NOT execute the full lock+update+insert path. If this
test runs on Sat/Sun UTC, the response codes will be 201 (passing the
assertion) but the throughput numbers measure the queuing path, not the
real transfer path. Run on a weekday for meaningful numbers.

Fraud signals at this scale: at 28 VUs × ~0.3 req/s = ~8 req/s
aggregate across 1000 source accounts, each source sees ~2-3 debits
per 5-min window — right at the VELOCITY_3_IN_5MIN trigger (40 pts).
VELOCITY_10_IN_1HR (30 pts) eventually fires after extended hold.
Total max 70 pts → REVIEW_FLAGGED (status 200, just flagged in the
fraud_signals table). Not a failure mode; just expected DB pressure
from the 10-min at-boundary hold.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/load/transfer.load.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/transfer-load.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/transfer-load-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:load:transfer

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts          (generates transfers.csv)
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
